ash for Debian and Ubuntu
=========================

Why this package needs a Python index at install time
-----------------------------------------------------

The package bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. Its post-install step creates
a virtualenv under /usr/lib/ash/venv and installs that wheel into it, which resolves
ASH's 27 runtime dependencies from whatever Python index the host is configured for.

This is not an oversight. ASH's own code may ship in a package published by the
project; third-party code may not. Carrying exactly one wheel is the guard that
keeps this package on the right side of that line, because it can be checked by
counting the files under /usr/lib/ash/wheels/ rather than by judging each
dependency one at a time.

Building a self-contained offline package for your own hosts is fine, and the
commands below are the supported way to do it. The line is about what the project
publishes, not about what the bytes are allowed to do.

On a host with no reachable index, stage one before installing. Resolve the
dependencies from ASH's own wheel -- the release asset, or the copy this package
installs under /usr/lib/ash/wheels/ -- on a machine that can reach an index. Do NOT
run `pip download automated-security-helper`: that name on PyPI belongs to an
unrelated third party, not to this project.

  pip download ./automated_security_helper-<version>-py3-none-any.whl -d /srv/ash-wheels
  # then, on the target, before installing the package:
  printf '[global]\nno-index = true\nfind-links = /srv/ash-wheels\n' > /etc/pip.conf

Where the post-install step fetches from
----------------------------------------

The package does not name an index. It runs pip with the host's own pip
configuration, so whatever that configuration says is where ASH's dependencies come
from: /etc/pip.conf, root's pip.conf (/root/.config/pip/pip.conf or
/root/.pip/pip.conf), and PIP_* variables such as PIP_INDEX_URL in the environment
the package manager runs in, since maintainer scripts inherit it. With no
configuration that is PyPI. This is deliberate: it is what lets the offline and
mirror setups above work. It also means anyone who can edit that configuration
decides what code the install step fetches and runs as root, so treat it with the
same care as an apt source.

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  ash dependencies install              # everything ASH can provision
  ash dependencies install --tool grype # or one at a time

Files
-----

  /usr/lib/ash/wheels/   ASH's wheel, the package's only payload
  /usr/lib/ash/venv      symlink to the live /usr/lib/ash/venv-<id>/, which
                         postinst creates; prerm removes both on `remove` only
  /usr/bin/ash           wrapper that execs the venv entry point
  /usr/share/doc/ash/    this file and the license (copyright)

The venv is built on the target rather than shipped, because a virtualenv records
absolute paths and the interpreter ABI of the machine that created it. On upgrade,
postinst builds the new venv in its own venv-<id> directory and only then renames a
new symlink over /usr/lib/ash/venv, so the command works throughout the upgrade, and
an upgrade that cannot reach an index leaves the previous version working. Re-run
the step once the index is reachable with:

  dpkg --configure -a
